Best-effort on invalid input. Box[T Stringer] makes Box[int] an illegal type, so
this source does not type-check (compile: false). plumb does not crash on it: the
constraint-violating demand Box[int] cannot be produced by Make and becomes an
injector input, while Make still serves the valid consumer at Box[Name]. The
generated signature faithfully reproduces the user’s own type error (Box[int]);
plumb neither rejects valid Go nor invents a fix. It passes the mistake through.
Contrast generic_constraint_input.txtar, the valid-type analog that compiles.
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
output: plumb_gen.go
compile: false
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type Stringer interface{ String() string }

type Name string

func (n Name) String() string { return string(n) }

type Box[T Stringer] struct{ V T }

//plumb:build
func Make[T Stringer]() Box[T] { return Box[T]{} }

//plumb:build
func UseGood(b Box[Name]) int { return 0 }

//plumb:build
func UseBad(b Box[int]) string { return "" }
